,\\,,\\,, the equation \(|x + y - 7| + |4x - y + 12| = 0\), because the absolute value of any real number is non-negative, the only way the sum of two absolute values can be zero is if both absolute values are zero. Therefore, we have the system of equations: \[
\begin{cases} x + y - 7 = 0 \\ 4x - y + 12 = 0 \end{cases}
\] Solving this system, we add the two equations to eliminate \(y\): \((x + y - 7) + (4x - y + 12) = 0 + 0 \implies 5x + 5 = 0 \implies x = -1\). Substituting \(x = -1\) into the first equation, we get: \(-1 + y - 7 = 0 \implies y = 8\). So the solution to the system of equations is \((x, y) = (-1, 8)\).</think> <answer> \((-1, 8)\) </answer>

